"Lessons learned from a 24-hour Hackathon (https://josy0ee-newsletter.beehiiv.com/p/lessons-learned-from-a-24-hour-hackathon)\nciteturn76view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://josy0ee-newsletter.beehiiv.com/p/lessons-learned-from-a-24-hour-hackathon\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 130\nL0:   * 【0†Home】\nL1:   * 【1†Posts】\nL2:   * Lessons learned from a 24-hour Hackathon\nL3: # Lessons learned from a 24-hour Hackathon\nL4: \nL5: 【2†Image: Jiaxin Zhang†media.beehiiv.com】\nL6: \nL7: 【3†Jiaxin Zhang】\nL8: \nL9: Apr 29, 2026\nL10: \nL11: •\nL12: \nL13: 4 min read\nL14: \nL15: I joined a 24-hour hackathon last weekend hosted by Whale x Anthropic, to best capture this experience, I’ll walk through it chronologically.\nL16: Before April 25, my friends(@Wei and @Eric) and I were told that we have been selected from over 700 applicants to participate. We started brainstorming ideas on the theme of “​Build local: think minorities, non-profits, municipalities, emergency services, or even countries.” We were drawn to public healthcare and anti-discrimination.\nL17: Lesson 1: Be intentional about why you join a hackathon.\nL18: Are you there to win? To have fun? To network? Time is your most limited resource during a hackathon, and your answer will shape every decision you make.\nL19: ## 25th April\nL20: \nL21: ##### 12:00\nL22: \nL23: We had 2 ideas on the table, one was a voice assistant for GP reception, allowing expats to desribe their systoms in their language and automatically generating personalized, structured health documents for GPs; another is was a one-stop anti-discrimination platform: collect → structure → report. Beyond documentation, it could also guide users on how to respond or take actions if needed.\nL24: ##### 17:00\nL25: \nL26: We initally chose the 1st idea because it felt more like a “winning” project. But after mapping out the flow and right before we started building, we discovered similar products on the market, so we decided to pivot and focus on the anti-discrimination solution.\nL27: \nL28: Lesson 2: It’s not necessary to build something “brand new” at a hackathon.\nL29: \nL30: Lesson 3: Always define a timeline before you start. Without it, discussions can easily become endless.\nL31: ##### 18:30\nL32: \nL33: We finialized the user flow and aligned on the functionality and scope.\nL34: \nL35: ##### 20:30\nL36: \nL37: We stepped out for a fancy dinner and networked. Then we start to feel a bit anxious after hearing some teams say they were almost done.\nL38: ##### 23:00\nL39: \nL40: Time went incredibly fast once we began building, we split the task and locked in: Wei on UI, Eric on Development while I do storytelling. However, even with “vibe coding”, problem is still everywhere.\nL41: \nL42: Q1: What did the solution look like?\nL43: A: We envisioned it as a mobile app.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nКак за 40 часов распознать всех поросят с помощью компьютерного зрения. Решение задачи на AgroCode Hack / Хабр (https://habr.com/ru/articles/653497/)\nciteturn76view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://habr.com/ru/articles/653497/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\nL0: 【0†Все потоки】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Войти】\nL3: \nL4: 【2†NapoleonIT】28 фев 2022 в 12:09\nL5: \nL6: Краткое содержание статьи\nL7: # Как за 40 часов распознать всех поросят с помощью компьютерного зрения. Решение задачи на AgroCode Hack\nL8: \nL9: 4 мин\nL10: \nL11: 4K\nL12: \nL13: 【3†Машинное обучение *】 【4†Обработка изображений *】 【5†Работа с видео *】 【6†Хакатоны】\nL14: \nL15: 【7†Image†habrastorage.org】\nL16: 2021 год был очень насыщенный на хакатоны и ИТ-чемпионаты. Мы неоднократно писали о том,  что специализируемся на проектах по компьютерному зрению. В этом наша сила. На AgroCode Hack нам предстояло за 40 часов решить кейс с использованием компьютерного зрения и AI по сегментации и отслеживанию поросят. Рассказываем, как в очередной раз заняли призовое место на хакатоне и отследили всех хрюш.\nL17: Привет, меня зовут Вова! И я Data Scientist в Napoleon IT. Недавно ребята из моего отдела писали статью про участие в хакатоне. Через неделю мы уже приняли участие в двух хакатонах и провели выходные в офисе тремя командами. В этот раз расскажу про участие в 【8†AgroCode Hack†agro-code.ru】 от Россельхозбанка. Наша команда LateDev состояла из 3 человек: 【9†я†github.com】 в роли Data Scientist, 【10†Ваня†github.com】 в роли Frontend Developer и 【11†Артур†github.com】 в роли Machine Learning Engineer.\nL18: Таким составом мы участвовали и раньше, и даже пару раз заняли первые места на Цифровом прорыве.\nL19: ### Подготовка к хакатону\nL20: \nL21: Из описания на сайте хакатона мы узнали, что нужно будет сегментировать и трекать поросят и как-то оценивать их активность, но что это значит - еще было непонятно. Также было неизвестно, как будет проверяться решение и по каким критериям оцениваться. Предупрежден - значит вооружен! Для страховки мы решили подготовить код обучения высокопроизводительного сегментатора. В итоге написали скрипт обучения 【12†SOLO†github.com】 на фреймворке mmdetection.\nL22: ### Постановка задач и ресурсы\nL23: Далее был технический созвон, на котором подробно рассказали о задаче и объяснили в каком виде эксперты ждут решение. Была возможность задать кейсосодержателю интересующие вопросы и уточнить некоторые моменты решения. Организаторы выделили всем участникам сервер с GPU nvidia tesla t4 и дали доступы. Суть задачи была в построении системы отслеживания и мониторинга активности животных в специальных “станках”.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\"Start Each feature with an Acceptance Test\" => end-to-end ? (https://groups.google.com/g/growing-object-oriented-software/c/uKtktAVhWLM)\nciteturn76view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn75view0\",\"pattern\":\"external\"}); Total lines: 1107\nL802: No! :-)\nL803: \nL804: You're missing the fundamental aspect TDD if you start by thinking up a load of layers and patterns, write tests that assert that code fits that design and then write code to fit into the design that's now concreted in by your tests.\nL805: Really try to start with the *smallest* test that you can.  Try to write the *simplest* implementation that makes the test pass.  Let structure emerge by refactoring.  As structure emerges choose meaningful names that expresses how the code relates what the system's users do and how they talk about the work. You may be surprised as to the design that emerges.  E.g. maybe there will be no classes named ThingyImpl, WotsitService or GizmoRepository.\nL806: For a start, how do your users or customer(s) refer to users of your system?  As a user base?  A user population?  Cohort? Community?  I bet they don't say \"User Repository\"!\nL807: The point of a walking skeleton is to help you understand the *external* integration points between your system (and team) and the other system's (and teams) you need to integrate with.  That may be third-party services, operations teams, network monitoring infrastructure mandated within the organisation, etc. etc. I would not include a database component that's fully under your control and that you deploy as part of your app in a walking skeleton.\nL808:  That's just an internal implementation choice that one can very often defer until later.\nL809: --Nat\nL810: \nL811: --\nL812: 【10†http://www.natpryce.com†www.natpryce.com】\nL813: \nL814: 【6†Image: Michael Azerhad's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com】\nL815: ### Michael Azerhad\nL816: \nL817: unread,\nL818: \nL819: Jun 18, 2013, 9:06:39 AM 6/18/13\nL820: \nL821: \nL822: \nL823: \nL824: \nL825: \nL826: \nL827: to growing-object-o...@googlegroups.com\nL828: \nL829: Understood for layers :) I will follow a pure TDD procedure so, letting my code to emerge them, if happens, without anticipating them :)\nL830: \nL831: And I agree, customers may not even know what is a database.. :)\nL832: Thus, why do we qualify the walking skeleton to be \"end-to-end\"? Should I rather deal with in-memory storage (simple POJO for instance) in order to be more flexible to future decision (deferring storage choices) if my TDD makes emerge the need for a storage?\nL833: \nL834: Why the choice of UI implementation, like GOOS presents with a Swing interface, is made at walking skeleton time? Why this choice should not be deferred, like database should?\nL835: \nL836: Can't I say: \"Customers may not even know about kind of UI interfaces\"?\nL837: By \"end-to-end\" terms, I understand the notion of concrete mechanisms…not really just a simple \"understanding\" of necessary components…\nL838: \nL839: I'm a bit confused so :)\nL840: \nL841: Thanks Nat :)\nL842: \nL843: \nL844: \nL845: 【23†Image: Ville Svärd's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com】\nL846: ### Ville Svärd\nL847: \nL848: unread,\nL849: \nL850: Jun 18, 2013, 9:09:39 AM 6/18/13\nL851: \nL852: \nL853: \nL854: \nL855: \nL856: \nL857: \nL858: to growing-object-o...@googlegroups.com\nL859: \nL860: Great answer, thanks for that Nat!\nL861: \nL862: --\nL863: \nL864: vs\nL865: \nL866: 2013/6/18 Nat Pryce <nat....@gmail.com>\nL867: \nL868: > \nL869: \nL870: > --\nL871: \nL872: >\nL873: > ---\nL874: > You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups \"Growing Object-Oriented Software\" group.\nL875: \nL876: > To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to growing-object-oriente...@googlegroups.com.\nL877: > For more options, visit 【11†https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out】.\nL878: >\nL879: >\nL880: \nL881: > --\nL882: vs.\nL883: \nL884: 【9†Image: Nat Pryce's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com】\nL885: ### Nat Pryce\nL886: \nL887: unread,\nL888: \nL889: Jun 18, 2013, 10:43:14 AM 6/18/13\nL890: \nL891: \nL892: \nL893: \nL894: \nL895: \nL896: \nL897: to growing-object-o...@googlegroups.com\nL898: \nL899: On 18 June 2013 14:06, Michael Azerhad <michael...@gmail.com> wrote:\nL900: \nL901: > Understood for layers :) I will follow a pure TDD procedure so, letting my code to emerge them, if happens, without anticipating them :)\nL902: >\nL903: > And I agree, customers may not even know what is a database.. :)\nL904: >\nL905: > Thus, why do we qualify the walking skeleton to be \"end-to-end\"?\nL935: >>> I make UserServiceImplTest passes easily and then UserControllerTest (containing a mocked UserServicesImplTest in order to be truly isolated)\nL936: >>>\nL937: >>> (I agree that a real TDD procedure doesn't know anything about layers at the very beginning... but in order to speed up a little, I allow me just this step jump.)\nL938: >>\nL939: >> No! :-)\nL940: >>\nL941: >> You're missing the fundamental aspect TDD if you start by thinking up a load of layers and patterns, write tests that assert that code fits that design and then write code to fit into the design that's now concreted in by your tests.\nL942: >>\nL943: >> Really try to start with the *smallest* test that you can.  Try to write the *simplest* implementation that makes the test pass.  Let structure emerge by refactoring.  As structure emerges choose meaningful names that expresses how the code relates what the system's users do and how they talk about the work. You may be surprised as to the design that emerges.  E.g. maybe there will be no classes named ThingyImpl, WotsitService or GizmoRepository.\nL944: >>\nL945: >> For a start, how do your users or customer(s) refer to users of your system?  As a user base?  A user population?  Cohort? Community?  I bet they don't say \"User Repository\"!\nL946: >>\nL947: >> The point of a walking skeleton is to help you understand the *external* integration points between your system (and team) and the other system's (and teams) you need to integrate with.  That may be third-party services, operations teams, network monitoring infrastructure mandated within the organisation, etc. etc. I would not include a database component that's fully under your control and that you deploy as part of your app in a walking skeleton.\nL948:  That's just an internal implementation choice that one can very often defer until later.\nL949: >>\nL950: >> --Nat\nL951: >>\nL952: >> --\nL953: >> 【10†http://www.natpryce.com†www.natpryce.com】\nL954: > --\nL955: \nL956: >\nL957: > ---\nL958: > You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups \"Growing Object-Oriented Software\" group.\nL959: \nL960: > To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to growing-object-oriente...@googlegroups.com.\nL961: \nL962: > For more options, visit 【11†https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out】.\nL963: >\nL964: >\nL965: \nL966: > --\nL967: 【10†http://www.natpryce.com†www.natpryce.com】\nL968: \nL969: 【6†Image: Michael Azerhad's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com】\nL970: ### Michael Azerhad\nL971: \nL972: unread,\nL973: \nL974: Jun 18, 2013, 11:03:06 AM 6/18/13\nL975: \nL976: \nL977: \nL978: \nL979: \nL980: \nL981: \nL982: to growing-object-o...@googlegroups.com\nL983: \nL984: I really appreciate your answer Nat :)\nL985: \nL986: Moreover, despite the early choice of UI (essential for customers feedback as you've just explained), I well imagine that PageObject pattern is there to help to mask the UI implementation and letting the possibility in the future to change this UI implementation, without breaking acceptance tests.\nL987: The more I practice TDD (recently), the more I enjoy it :)  However, a lot of huge companies, like investment banking would just say (I lived it):  \"Code quickly, dirty and shut up, we have already QA to test\" ...  Ietting GOOS practice to only home. Frustrating :)\nL988: \nL989: Michael\nL990: \nL991: \nL992: \nL993: \nL994: \nL995: \nL996: \nL997: \nL998: \nL999: \nL1000: \nL1001: > > To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to growing-object-oriented-software+unsubscribe@googlegroups.com.\nL1002: > > For more options, visit 【11†https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out】.\nL1003: >>\nL1004: >>\nL1005: \nL1006: > > --\nL1007: > 【10†http://www.natpryce.com†www.natpryce.com】\nL1008: \nL1009: 【24†Image: Lance Walton's profile photo†lh3.googleusercontent.com】\nL1010: ### Lance Walton\nL1011: \nL1012: unread,\nL1013: \nL1014: Jun 18, 2013, 11:21:52 AM 6/18/13\nL1015: \nL1016: \nL1017: \nL1018: \nL1019: \nL1020: \nL1021: \nL1022: to growing-object-o...@googlegroups.com\nL1023: \nL1024: On 18 Jun 2013, at 16:03, Michael Azerhad <michael...@gmail.com> wrote:\nL1025: > However, a lot of huge companies, like investment banking would just say (I lived it): \"Code quickly, dirty and shut up, we have already QA to test\" ... Ietting GOOS practice to only home. Frustrating :)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChapter 10. The Walking Skeleton - Growing Object-Oriented Software, Guided by Tests [Book] (https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/growing-object-oriented-software/9780321574442/ch10.html)\nciteturn76view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn75view1\",\"pattern\":\"Chapter 10.\"}); Total lines: 524\nL338: # Growing Object-Oriented Software, Guided by Tests\nL339: \nL340: ##### by 【122†Steve Freeman】, 【123†Nat Pryce】\nL341: \nL342: October 2009\nL343: \nL344: Intermediate to advanced\nL345: \nL346: 384 pages\nL347: \nL348: 8h 54m\nL349: \nL350: English\nL351: \nL352: 【124†Addison-Wesley Professional】\nL353: \nL354: 【125†Read now Unlock full access】\nL355: ### Contents\nL356: \nL357:   * ### 【126†Title Page】\nL358: \nL359: Copyright Page Contents Praise for Growing Object-Oriented Software, Guided by Tests Foreword Preface Acknowledgments About the Authors\nL360: \nL361:   * ### 【127†Part I. Introduction】\nL362: \nL363:   * ### 【128†Chapter 1. What Is the Point of Test-Driven Development?】\nL364: \nL365:   * ### 【129†Chapter 2. Test-Driven Development with Objects】\nL366: \nL367:   * ### 【130†Chapter 3. An Introduction to the Tools】\nL368: \nL369:   * ### 【131†Part II. The Process of Test-Driven Development】\nL370:   * ### 【132†Chapter 4. Kick-Starting the Test-Driven Cycle】\nL371: \nL372:   * ### 【133†Chapter 5. Maintaining the Test-Driven Cycle】\nL373: \nL374:   * ### 【134†Chapter 6. Object-Oriented Style】\nL375: \nL376:   * ### 【135†Chapter 7. Achieving Object-Oriented Design】\nL377: \nL378:   * ### 【136†Chapter 8. Building on Third-Party Code】\nL379: \nL380:   * ### 【137†Part III. A Worked Example】\nL381: \nL382:   * ### 【138†Chapter 9. Commissioning an Auction Sniper】\nL383: \nL384:   * ### 【139†Chapter 10. The Walking Skeleton】\nL385: \nL386:   * ### 【140†Chapter 11. Passing the First Test】\nL387:   * ### 【141†Chapter 12. Getting Ready to Bid】\nL388: \nL389:   * ### 【142†Chapter 13. The Sniper Makes a Bid】\nL390: \nL391:   * ### 【143†Chapter 14. The Sniper Wins the Auction】\nL392: \nL393:   * ### 【144†Chapter 15. Towards a Real User Interface】\nL394: \nL395:   * ### 【145†Chapter 16. Sniping for Multiple Items】\nL396: \nL397:   * ### 【146†Chapter 17. Teasing Apart Main】\nL398: \nL399:   * ### 【147†Chapter 18. Filling In the Details】\nL400: \nL401:   * ### 【148†Chapter 19. Handling Failure】\nL402: \nL403:   * ### 【149†Part IV. Sustainable Test-Driven Development】\nL404:   * ### 【150†Chapter 20. Listening to the Tests】\nL405: \nL406:   * ### 【151†Chapter 21. Test Readability】\nL407: \nL408:   * ### 【152†Chapter 22. Constructing Complex Test Data】\nL409: \nL410:   * ### 【153†Chapter 23. Test Diagnostics】\nL411: \nL412:   * ### 【154†Chapter 24. Test Flexibility】\nL413: \nL414:   * ### 【155†Part V. Advanced Topics】\nL415: \nL416:   * ### 【156†Chapter 25. Testing Persistence】\nL417: \nL418:   * ### 【157†Chapter 26. Unit Testing and Threads】\nL419: \nL420:   * ### 【158†Chapter 27. Testing Asynchronous Code】\nL421: \nL422:   * ### 【159†Afterword. A Brief History of Mock Objects】\nL423:   * ### 【160†Appendix A. jMock2 Cheat Sheet】\nL424: \nL425:   * ### 【161†Appendix B. Writing a Hamcrest Matcher】\nL426: \nL427:   * ### 【162†Bibliography】\nL428: \nL429:   * ### 【163†Index】\nL430: \nL431: [Button: Show More]\nL432: \nL433: Content preview from 【164†Growing Object-Oriented Software, Guided by Tests】\nL434: ## Chapter 10. The Walking Skeleton\nL435: \nL436: In which we set up our development environment and write our first end-to-end test. We make some infrastructure choices that allow us to get started, and construct a build. We’re surprised, yet again, at how much effort this takes.\nL437: ### Get the Skeleton out of the Closet\nL438: \nL439: So now we’ve got an idea of what to build, can we get on with it and write our first unit test?\nL440: \nL441: Not yet.\nL442: \nL443: Our first task is to create the “walking skeleton” we described in “【165†First, Test a Walking Skeleton】” (page 【165†32】). Again, the point of the walking skeleton is to help us understand the requirements well enough to propose and validate a broad-brush system structure. We can always change our minds later, when we learn more, but it’s important to start ...\nL444: ##### Become an O’Reilly member and get unlimited access to this title plus top books and audiobooks from O’Reilly and nearly 200 top publishers, thousands of courses curated by job role, 150+ live events each month,\nL445: and much more.\nL446: \nL447: 【125†Read now Unlock full access】\nL448: ### More than 5,000 organizations count on O’Reilly\nL449: \nL450: 【166†Image: AirBnb】【167†Image: BlueOrigin】【168†Image: Electronic Arts】【169†Image: HomeDepot】【170†Image: Nasdaq】【171†Image: Rakuten】【172†Image: Tata Consultancy Services】\nL451: \nL452: * * *\nL453: \nL454: 【173†Image: QuotationMark】O’Reilly covers everything we've got, with content to help us build a world-class technology community, upgrade the capabilities and competencies of our teams, and improve overall team performance as well as their engagement.\nL455: ##### Julian F.\nL456: \nL457: Head of Cybersecurity\nL458: \nL459: 【173†Image: QuotationMark】I wanted to learn C and C++, but it didn't click for me until I picked up an O'Reilly book. When I went on the O’Reilly platform, I was astonished to find all the books there, plus live events and sandboxes so you could play around with the technology.\nL460: ##### Addison B.\nL461: \nL462: Field Engineer\nL463: \nL464: 【173†Image: QuotationMark】I’ve been on the O’Reilly platform for more than eight years. I use a couple of learning platforms, but I'm on O'Reilly more than anybody else. When you're there, you start learning. I'm never disappointed.\nL465: ##### Amir M.\nL466: \nL467: Data Platform Tech Lead\nL468: \nL469: 【173†Image: QuotationMark】I'm always learning. So when I got on to O'Reilly, I was like a kid in a candy store. There are playlists. There are answers. There's on-demand training. It's worth its weight in gold, in terms of what it allows me to do.\nL470: \nL471: ##### Mark W.\nL472: \nL473: Embedded Software Engineer\nL474: ### You might also like\nL475: \nL476: 【174†Effective Software Testing】Mauricio Aniche\nL477: \nL478: 【175†Modern Software Engineering: Doing What Works to Build Better Software Faster】David Farley\nL479: \nL480: 【176†Unit Testing Principles, Practices, and Patterns】Vladimir Khorikov\nL481: \nL482: 【177†Test Driven Development: By Example】Kent Beck\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\npubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12683469/?dopt=Abstract)\nciteturn76view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn72view0\",\"pattern\":\"Abstract\"}); Total lines: 1\nNo matching text found for \"Abstract\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEffects of total sleep deprivation on 